1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,000
The gun has played a critical role in history.

2
00:00:05,001 --> 00:00:10,000
An invention which has been praised and denounced, served hero and villain alike,

3
00:00:10,001 --> 00:00:13,000
and carries with it moral responsibility.

4
00:00:13,001 --> 00:00:17,000
To understand the gun is to better understand history.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:44,000
On a secluded corner of a U.S. military depot in rural Alabama

6
00:00:45,400 --> 00:00:48,080
stands a gray blockhouse structure,

7
00:00:49,080 --> 00:00:51,340
imposing if only for its anonymity.

8
00:00:54,040 --> 00:00:57,280
But behind these walls of reinforced concrete

9
00:00:57,281 --> 00:00:59,920
exists an enterprise like no other,

10
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:01,400
in the Department of Defense.

11
00:01:03,600 --> 00:01:08,640
This climate-controlled facility belongs to the center of military history.

12
00:01:09,540 --> 00:01:13,320
It is, in fact, the official attic of the United States Army.

13
00:01:14,640 --> 00:01:18,020
An attic filled with 200 years of history,

14
00:01:19,320 --> 00:01:21,100
100,000 artifacts,

15
00:01:22,160 --> 00:01:24,680
uniforms, flags, artillery,

16
00:01:25,480 --> 00:01:26,900
tanks, jeeps,

17
00:01:26,901 --> 00:01:30,800
and 17,000 fully operational guns.

18
00:01:39,940 --> 00:01:44,380
Our purpose is to ship, store, and receive weapons for the Army Museum system.

19
00:01:45,580 --> 00:01:47,360
These are fully operable weapons,

20
00:01:47,820 --> 00:01:50,440
so every time we open a box to pull weapons for our shipment,

21
00:01:50,680 --> 00:01:53,280
it's inventoryed before it's put back on the shelf.

22
00:01:56,900 --> 00:02:03,020
These ordinary wooden crates hold extraordinary contents.

23
00:02:06,020 --> 00:02:08,440
Thompson submachine guns.

24
00:02:09,840 --> 00:02:13,600
.30 caliber and .50 caliber machine guns.

25
00:02:15,420 --> 00:02:17,860
.45 caliber pistols.

26
00:02:17,861 --> 00:02:27,900
some of these weapons from world war ii are in mint condition still in the original factory boxes

27
00:02:27,901 --> 00:02:37,780
the center of military history demands accuracy ensuring that the correct model of gun is used

28
00:02:37,781 --> 00:02:44,380
in a particular display is of vital importance to what goes on here these weapons are laid out

29
00:02:44,381 --> 00:02:46,780
going to various museums in the field,

30
00:02:47,240 --> 00:02:49,560
and we go through these weapons

31
00:02:49,561 --> 00:02:52,820
and ensure that they get the proper type for the time period portrayed.

32
00:02:53,580 --> 00:02:56,000
And in some cases, they may cover several war periods.

33
00:02:57,200 --> 00:03:01,040
These are M1 carbines that were used through World War II, Korea, and Vietnam.

34
00:03:01,280 --> 00:03:05,220
The earlier model had the flip sight and the flat bolt.

35
00:03:06,060 --> 00:03:08,620
Both models would have seen service in subsequent wars,

36
00:03:08,700 --> 00:03:11,840
but only the one with the flip sight would be accurate for World War II.

37
00:03:11,841 --> 00:03:16,920
even though they are the same stock number and would be issued indiscriminately,

38
00:03:17,040 --> 00:03:18,580
they are very different for our purposes.

39
00:03:19,920 --> 00:03:22,240
Though these guns are silent now,

40
00:03:22,560 --> 00:03:26,060
they remain a palpable link to that not-too-distant past

41
00:03:26,061 --> 00:03:30,880
when brave men carried them into battle and changed the world forever.

42
00:03:32,560 --> 00:03:37,600
But to understand the weapons, one must first understand the infantry itself.

43
00:03:37,601 --> 00:03:47,660
Prior to entering World War II, the United States Army adopted a concept of offense based on fire and maneuver,

44
00:03:48,560 --> 00:03:52,440
relying on the knock-down power of the .30 caliber weapons then in use.

45
00:03:55,000 --> 00:04:01,940
At its most basic level, fire and maneuver meant that the enemy would be fired upon or fixed by one group,

46
00:04:02,360 --> 00:04:05,040
then maneuvered against and destroyed by a second.

47
00:04:05,041 --> 00:04:12,040
Army organization revolved around this concept.

48
00:04:14,040 --> 00:04:20,040
The Basic Infantry Rifle Company comprised 187 soldiers and six officers.

49
00:04:20,041 --> 00:04:23,040
It was organized into four platoons.

50
00:04:23,041 --> 00:04:28,040
Three were rifle platoons, which had three squads each.

51
00:04:28,041 --> 00:04:34,040
The fourth was a weapons platoon, which provided the heavy firepower.

52
00:04:35,040 --> 00:04:39,420
Typically, three machine guns, three mortars, and three bazookas.

53
00:04:41,220 --> 00:04:47,620
As the rifle platoons moved forward, the weapons platoon would move in close support

54
00:04:47,621 --> 00:04:51,280
and then would be directed by the company commander to the sector

55
00:04:51,281 --> 00:04:53,960
where additional firepower was needed the most.

56
00:04:54,820 --> 00:04:59,400
Another way they were often used is they were divided up, again, with the organization of three.

57
00:04:59,980 --> 00:05:04,820
Each platoon might get a machine gun, a mortar, or a bazooka.

58
00:05:05,040 --> 00:05:10,660
But the key thing that the riflemen looked for was once they had located the enemy and fixed him,

59
00:05:11,120 --> 00:05:15,300
that additional support was readily available from these heavier weapons.

60
00:05:17,120 --> 00:05:19,120
You get as close as you can without getting hit.

61
00:05:20,500 --> 00:05:21,920
You had to draw them off.

62
00:05:22,040 --> 00:05:24,720
You'd draw the fire one way and somebody else would come in another way.

63
00:05:25,840 --> 00:05:28,160
Close contact to them as close as you could.

64
00:05:28,700 --> 00:05:30,180
And you had to worm your way up to it.

65
00:05:30,181 --> 00:05:38,580
The rifle and weapons platoons, 36 men each, were called Alpha Bravo teams.

66
00:05:39,500 --> 00:05:46,000
Throughout the war, it was these men who would carry the ultimate burden of closing with and destroying the enemy.

67
00:05:49,000 --> 00:05:52,940
You're watching U.S. Guns of World War II on Modern Marvels.

68
00:05:55,220 --> 00:05:59,140
The rifle has long been the traditional weapon of the infantrymen.

69
00:06:00,180 --> 00:06:06,400
The adoption of fire and maneuver tactics induced a transition from bolt action to semi-automatic rifles.

70
00:06:08,080 --> 00:06:12,257
Although the M1 Garan would become the standard rifle of the U.S.

71
00:06:12,258 --> 00:06:15,920
Army during the war, initially they were in short supply.

72
00:06:17,700 --> 00:06:23,173
Thousands of older model rifles remained in use, such as the U.S.

73
00:06:23,174 --> 00:06:28,140
rifle caliber .30 M1903, commonly known as the Springfield.

74
00:06:28,800 --> 00:06:32,020
The design was licensed from the Mauser Company of Germany

75
00:06:32,021 --> 00:06:36,320
after the Spanish-American War for approximately $200,000.

76
00:06:38,520 --> 00:06:42,433
This is a 1903 Springfield, which was the standard U.S.

77
00:06:42,433 --> 00:06:44,780
Army rifle in the interwar years.

78
00:06:45,560 --> 00:06:51,360
The Springfield is a bolt-action, turn-bolt-operated, magazine-fed,

79
00:06:52,760 --> 00:06:55,540
infantry rifle, has about a 26-inch barrel.

80
00:06:55,541 --> 00:06:58,860
the sights on this rifle were horrible.

81
00:07:00,420 --> 00:07:02,240
It has a little notch rear sight.

82
00:07:03,360 --> 00:07:05,520
For long-range work, the sight's raised.

83
00:07:06,160 --> 00:07:08,860
It's adjusted by turning this through and moving it up and down.

84
00:07:08,980 --> 00:07:10,660
There's an aperture for that work.

85
00:07:11,460 --> 00:07:14,840
It's fine for range work and precision shooting on the range,

86
00:07:15,260 --> 00:07:18,020
but in combat, your targets are very fast-moving,

87
00:07:18,021 --> 00:07:22,320
and you have to be able to very quickly acquire them, take aim, and then shoot.

88
00:07:22,321 --> 00:07:33,140
Because of the shortage of combat rifles early in the war, production of the Model 1903, or 03 as it was called, was reactivated.

89
00:07:33,640 --> 00:07:40,140
When the Marines landed on Guadalcanal in 1942, most were armed with Springfields.

90
00:07:40,141 --> 00:07:48,720
Nearly two years later, on June 6th, 1944,

91
00:07:49,660 --> 00:07:54,580
Spec 5 Harry Davis stormed ashore at Normandy with the 5th Ranger Infantry Battalion.

92
00:07:55,340 --> 00:08:01,360
As a ranger, Davis had been trained to use every type of small arm found in the European theater.

93
00:08:02,120 --> 00:08:05,680
But he had a particular fondness for the O3A4.

94
00:08:05,681 --> 00:08:10,340
I was the company commander's bodyguard

95
00:08:10,341 --> 00:08:12,020
and company sniper

96
00:08:12,021 --> 00:08:14,340
and wherever he went, I went

97
00:08:14,341 --> 00:08:16,920
the 03 was a sniper weapon

98
00:08:16,921 --> 00:08:20,080
that I used to hit targets

99
00:08:20,081 --> 00:08:23,400
that were far away and with precision

100
00:08:23,401 --> 00:08:26,980
and they were very, very accurate guns

101
00:08:26,981 --> 00:08:30,660
it's more accurate because of a rigid construction

102
00:08:30,661 --> 00:08:32,920
there is no moving parts when the gun is fired

103
00:08:32,921 --> 00:08:34,020
other than the firing pin

104
00:08:34,021 --> 00:08:42,760
The 1903A4 was equipped with a telescopic sight, was issued primarily to snipers for very accurate fire at 800 to 1,000 yards.

105
00:08:44,420 --> 00:08:50,780
Harry Davis once employed his sniper skills to deal with German harassment from across no man's land.

106
00:08:52,160 --> 00:08:56,400
There was a gully between this and that, maybe about 200 yards.

107
00:08:58,080 --> 00:09:00,600
And there was a well there.

108
00:09:01,040 --> 00:09:02,960
And when we went to the well, it creaked.

109
00:09:02,961 --> 00:09:07,740
and every time somebody went to the well to get water this german would fire on it

110
00:09:07,741 --> 00:09:13,360
and hit somebody and i made my mind if i was going to get the guy that's doing it

111
00:09:13,361 --> 00:09:18,640
i'll repay them back i laid there for about four or five hours and i saw somebody come out with a

112
00:09:18,641 --> 00:09:26,280
white shirt a t-shirt and hang his clothes on the line a white shirt or a white t-shirt again

113
00:09:26,281 --> 00:09:32,920
and i laid there and i zeroed in with my o3 rifle and i measured it measured it measured

114
00:09:32,921 --> 00:09:36,360
and lo and behold, he came out to get his shirt off the line and I fired.

115
00:09:37,320 --> 00:09:39,400
We didn't have any problems with the well anymore.

116
00:09:40,560 --> 00:09:44,560
The Springfield remained in service as a sniper weapon throughout the war.

117
00:09:45,060 --> 00:09:48,940
But the next generation infantry rifle was already in production.

118
00:09:50,420 --> 00:09:53,780
In 1937, after a decade of development,

119
00:09:54,340 --> 00:10:00,880
the U.S. Army had officially accepted the gas-operated semi-automatic caliber 30 M1

120
00:10:00,881 --> 00:10:03,020
designed by John Garin.

121
00:10:07,180 --> 00:10:09,840
John Cantius Garin was actually a Canadian.

122
00:10:10,460 --> 00:10:13,660
And he was a classic, what the British would refer to as eccentric.

123
00:10:14,300 --> 00:10:16,940
He kept an ice skating rink in the living room of his home

124
00:10:16,941 --> 00:10:19,500
and would go ice skate in his house.

125
00:10:20,120 --> 00:10:21,260
But the man was a genius.

126
00:10:22,280 --> 00:10:25,240
Probably the most successful rifle of all time

127
00:10:25,241 --> 00:10:28,980
in terms of actual troop use in combat was the M1 Garin.

128
00:10:28,981 --> 00:10:34,440
And Garen's genius was such that he overcame every obstacle that was placed in his way.

129
00:10:36,860 --> 00:10:42,020
The rifle was reliable under virtually every environment into which it was subjected.

130
00:10:42,660 --> 00:10:46,720
I've talked to some World War II veterans from the Battle of the Boas in Hurricane Forest

131
00:10:46,721 --> 00:10:51,880
when the rifle was frozen, literally, and they made the rifle go again by just urinating on it,

132
00:10:52,280 --> 00:10:53,520
and it was fine after that.

133
00:10:53,521 --> 00:11:04,520
the m1 fired an eight round clip a trained rifleman could empty it within 20 seconds

134
00:11:04,521 --> 00:11:08,760
with unparalleled accuracy for such a high rate of fire

135
00:11:08,761 --> 00:11:17,020
the reason for that was because of the gas system of the m1 the gas system caused the recoil to be

136
00:11:17,021 --> 00:11:21,847
softened, and as a consequence of that, it lessened muzzle rise.

137
00:11:21,848 --> 00:11:24,260
The rifleman was able to get his

138
00:11:24,261 --> 00:11:27,927
follow-up shots off much more quickly than he could with a bolt-action gun.

139
00:11:27,928 --> 00:11:29,540
For one thing, with a bolt-action

140
00:11:29,541 --> 00:11:32,945
gun, between each shot, you're having to work that bolt.

141
00:11:32,946 --> 00:11:35,620
You have to get your face up out of the way,

142
00:11:35,900 --> 00:11:38,160
you lose your sight picture, you lose your position.

143
00:11:38,161 --> 00:11:49,900
but in the end a weapon was only as effective as the man who carried it transforming a nation of

144
00:11:49,901 --> 00:11:56,040
farmers and accountants factory workers and bus drivers into soldiers was a daunting challenge

145
00:11:56,041 --> 00:12:04,480
for many young men like bill raines from los angeles california the garan was the first

146
00:12:04,481 --> 00:12:07,640
rifle they had ever held, let alone fired.

147
00:12:08,680 --> 00:12:12,160
The Marine Corps has one particular way of training you to fire.

148
00:12:12,820 --> 00:12:19,140
The four basic positions was the prone, the kneeling, the sitting, and the standing.

149
00:12:19,620 --> 00:12:23,120
And we would go through these four positions over and over and over.

150
00:12:24,260 --> 00:12:29,820
But hours spent on the rifle range did not necessarily translate into combat effectiveness.

151
00:12:30,820 --> 00:12:35,100
Sometimes, stateside procedures had to be untaught in the war zone.

152
00:12:36,440 --> 00:12:42,120
One of the important aspects of American training which was criticized during and after the war

153
00:12:42,121 --> 00:12:46,300
was the way that infantrymen were trained with their basic weapons.

154
00:12:47,280 --> 00:12:53,100
That training environment had very little to do with what the soldier faced in actual combat.

155
00:12:53,980 --> 00:12:57,240
In combat, there was the noise and confusion of the battlefield,

156
00:12:57,241 --> 00:12:59,940
terrible weather conditions

157
00:12:59,941 --> 00:13:02,100
all kinds of obstructions

158
00:13:02,101 --> 00:13:03,260
and the enemy

159
00:13:03,261 --> 00:13:05,820
made it their business

160
00:13:05,821 --> 00:13:07,800
not to be seen on the battlefield.

161
00:13:09,120 --> 00:13:10,280
The American soldier

162
00:13:10,281 --> 00:13:11,120
had a tendency

163
00:13:11,121 --> 00:13:12,680
not to shoot

164
00:13:12,681 --> 00:13:14,600
at something he could not see.

165
00:13:15,480 --> 00:13:16,880
His trainers in the States

166
00:13:16,881 --> 00:13:18,760
told him this was a waste of ammunition.

167
00:13:19,940 --> 00:13:21,460
When soldiers got in battle

168
00:13:21,461 --> 00:13:23,040
the veterans told him

169
00:13:23,041 --> 00:13:24,160
the exact opposite.

170
00:13:24,940 --> 00:13:26,220
Get your weapon out.

171
00:13:26,760 --> 00:13:29,360
We know the enemy is in a certain location,

172
00:13:29,940 --> 00:13:32,720
and we as a squad, as a platoon or a company,

173
00:13:32,820 --> 00:13:37,520
need to generate a volume of fire against this objective

174
00:13:37,521 --> 00:13:40,300
that will allow us to maneuver against it.

175
00:13:40,820 --> 00:13:43,780
So don't worry about whether you can see the enemy or not.

176
00:13:44,300 --> 00:13:47,520
And they told their soldiers, we can always get more ammunition.

177
00:13:47,521 --> 00:13:49,880
We may not be able to get more riflemen.

178
00:13:51,680 --> 00:13:54,300
Whatever the shortcomings of stateside training,

179
00:13:54,301 --> 00:14:01,260
In combat, the Garand M1 rifle was the consummate integration of technology and tactics.

180
00:14:02,740 --> 00:14:05,777
No less in authority than General George S.

181
00:14:05,778 --> 00:14:10,580
Patton III called the M1 the greatest battle implement ever devised.

182
00:14:12,840 --> 00:14:18,420
The submachine gun, although used by the Germans in World War I in limited numbers,

183
00:14:18,900 --> 00:14:22,460
came to prominence as a combat weapon in the next great war.

184
00:14:23,460 --> 00:14:34,586
Using small caliber pistol ammunition, the submachine gun combined the machine gun's high volume of fire with the mobility of the rifle at the expense of range and

185
00:14:34,587 --> 00:14:35,287
accuracy.

186
00:14:37,780 --> 00:14:40,001
At the end of World War I, the U.S.

187
00:14:40,002 --> 00:14:43,300
Army showed little interest in pursuing the concept.

188
00:14:43,680 --> 00:14:47,165
It fell to one man, retired Army General John T.

189
00:14:47,166 --> 00:14:52,320
Thompson, to develop and promote the trench broom gun for close combat.

190
00:14:52,460 --> 00:15:05,460
Thompson later changed the name to submachine gun, which meant any small automatic firing weapon chambered for the .45 caliber automatic Colt pistol, or ACP.

191
00:15:06,460 --> 00:15:13,460
The Tommy gun, as it became known, first sold in quantity to the civilian market as the Model 1921.

192
00:15:14,460 --> 00:15:19,460
It gained notoriety during the Prohibition era as the preferred weapon of the mob.

193
00:15:22,460 --> 00:15:27,840
The Thompson's early association with the gangster element poisoned relations with the military.

194
00:15:28,800 --> 00:15:33,900
Finally, in 1927, the U.S. Marines obtained several hundred of the weapons

195
00:15:33,901 --> 00:15:38,980
and used them to good effect in the jungles of Nicaragua, subduing Sandino guerrillas.

196
00:15:41,200 --> 00:15:46,760
The Navy Department officially adopted a modified Thompson submachine gun in 1928.

197
00:15:47,120 --> 00:15:51,460
The rate of fire was reduced from 800 to 600 rounds per minute.

198
00:15:52,460 --> 00:15:57,900
The Cutts Compensator, which lessened muzzle climb by deflecting the blast, was standardized.

199
00:16:02,100 --> 00:16:05,860
This is the model 1928 Thompson submachine gun.

200
00:16:05,960 --> 00:16:09,020
It's a full automatic .45 caliber weapon.

201
00:16:09,300 --> 00:16:19,020
This particular model has the Cutts Compensator, can fire the 50-round drum magazine or 30-round box magazine, and has a multi-piece bolt.

202
00:16:20,000 --> 00:16:21,860
The sights on the weapon are very intricate.

203
00:16:22,460 --> 00:16:24,180
and be used minutely.

204
00:16:24,820 --> 00:16:27,060
These were first purchased by the post office

205
00:16:27,061 --> 00:16:29,440
and later used by the Marine Corps in the Caribbean.

206
00:16:31,220 --> 00:16:33,200
With Europe on the brink of war,

207
00:16:33,460 --> 00:16:37,040
the U.S. Army finally placed an order for over 20,000 guns.

208
00:16:38,980 --> 00:16:42,540
It became abundantly clear that we needed submachine guns,

209
00:16:43,060 --> 00:16:44,760
and the only thing we had was the Thompson.

210
00:16:45,460 --> 00:16:49,240
The Thompson was a very complicated submachine gun to manufacture.

211
00:16:49,241 --> 00:16:56,240
The Thompson was redesigned in 1941 to simplify production and reduce costs.

212
00:16:56,241 --> 00:17:01,240
The following year, it was standardized as the M1 submachine gun.

213
00:17:01,241 --> 00:17:08,240
The later model M1 and M1A1 don't have the cuts compensator,

214
00:17:08,241 --> 00:17:14,240
fire only from the box magazine, and have a very simplistic sheet metal sight.

215
00:17:15,020 --> 00:17:19,600
These weapons were about $350 to produce during World War II.

216
00:17:21,000 --> 00:17:27,000
A Thompson submachine gun isn't something that you hold up like an M1 and sight through and squeeze off around.

217
00:17:27,340 --> 00:17:29,320
It lays down a volume of fire.

218
00:17:29,980 --> 00:17:35,860
And when you get up and you want to move out, you want a volume laid down, a spraying effect.

219
00:17:36,000 --> 00:17:38,940
It's going to keep the enemy's head down while you get up and go.

220
00:17:38,941 --> 00:17:44,940
Thompson's were carefully allocated to squad leaders and NCOs

221
00:17:44,941 --> 00:17:46,860
except in ranger battalions

222
00:17:46,861 --> 00:17:51,280
anybody could have a Thompson that could use it

223
00:17:51,281 --> 00:17:54,720
and if a squad leader went down he picked it up it was his weapon

224
00:17:54,721 --> 00:17:59,020
and the submachine guns were anybody's weapon

225
00:17:59,021 --> 00:18:00,640
at that time

226
00:18:00,641 --> 00:18:04,940
we were attached to the 29th Infantry Division

227
00:18:04,941 --> 00:18:08,320
and we spearheaded anything they ran into trouble with

228
00:18:08,321 --> 00:18:12,980
If they hit sniper fire out of trees, rangers up front.

229
00:18:13,400 --> 00:18:16,780
Rangers had to come in and rake the trees and knock the sniper out.

230
00:18:17,480 --> 00:18:22,940
Or if they ran into a difficulty going into a little town, a house or a pillbox, rangers up front.

231
00:18:24,340 --> 00:18:30,120
In the Pacific Theater, there never were enough Thompsons to go around, at least for the Marines.

232
00:18:31,220 --> 00:18:36,960
The Thompson submachine gun was probably the most prized weapon in the infantry.

233
00:18:36,961 --> 00:18:43,720
It wasn't issued, and those that had them, we never asked how they came by them to get them.

234
00:18:45,740 --> 00:18:49,180
Although heavy, expensive, and complex to manufacture,

235
00:18:49,960 --> 00:18:54,000
the Tommy gun had one great overriding virtue, its reliability.

236
00:18:55,020 --> 00:18:59,300
By 1945, over one and a quarter million had been produced.

237
00:19:01,660 --> 00:19:06,360
Nevertheless, the Army began looking for a less expensive alternative to the Thompson

238
00:19:06,361 --> 00:19:08,240
as early as 1941.

239
00:19:09,900 --> 00:19:12,500
Even when it was simplified for mass production,

240
00:19:12,620 --> 00:19:15,340
the Thompson was still horribly complicated to manufacture.

241
00:19:16,200 --> 00:19:17,660
It required a lot of machining,

242
00:19:18,200 --> 00:19:19,240
was very expensive,

243
00:19:19,780 --> 00:19:21,940
and we needed submachine guns.

244
00:19:24,500 --> 00:19:26,500
In October 1942,

245
00:19:27,040 --> 00:19:28,580
the U.S. Army began development

246
00:19:28,581 --> 00:19:30,960
of a stamped sheet metal submachine gun

247
00:19:30,961 --> 00:19:33,160
based on the British Sten design.

248
00:19:36,360 --> 00:19:43,580
Within two months, the Army had formally adopted the submachine gun, caliber .45 M3.

249
00:19:47,980 --> 00:19:54,700
It was immediately nicknamed the Grease Gun because of its resemblance to that particular garage implement.

250
00:19:55,760 --> 00:19:59,280
This is the .45 caliber submachine gun M3.

251
00:19:59,920 --> 00:20:02,680
It's made out of mostly stamped and welded parts.

252
00:20:03,120 --> 00:20:04,500
It can be made very cheaply.

253
00:20:06,360 --> 00:20:13,540
The safety is a small metal tab that fits into a hole in the bolt that keeps the cocking lever from moving.

254
00:20:14,200 --> 00:20:17,180
These cost about $15 to produce during World War II.

255
00:20:19,000 --> 00:20:22,840
The Army contracted with the Guidelamp Division of General Motors,

256
00:20:23,300 --> 00:20:27,200
which had a lot of experience making stamped headlight housings for cars.

257
00:20:28,280 --> 00:20:31,740
M3 production was underway by the summer of 1943.

258
00:20:31,741 --> 00:20:39,000
The most complicated machining part of it is the bolt, which is very heavy.

259
00:20:39,580 --> 00:20:46,360
Works on two recoil springs and straight blowback, and it fires at a rate of about 400 rounds a minute.

260
00:20:47,000 --> 00:20:48,720
.45 automatic caliber.

261
00:20:49,640 --> 00:20:50,580
Simplicity itself.

262
00:20:51,660 --> 00:20:58,360
In 1944, the M3 was modified to allow for even greater ease of production and use in the field.

263
00:20:59,080 --> 00:21:02,800
This new grease gun was adopted in December 1944

264
00:21:02,801 --> 00:21:08,140
as the submachine gun caliber .45 M3A1.

265
00:21:10,340 --> 00:21:13,560
This is the M3A1 submachine gun.

266
00:21:14,000 --> 00:21:18,480
New in the box, it contains the magazines wrapped in foil paper

267
00:21:18,481 --> 00:21:20,840
and the barrel wrapped in foil paper,

268
00:21:20,841 --> 00:21:23,520
the original packaging of the weapon from the factory.

269
00:21:23,521 --> 00:21:29,560
A particularly ingenious feature of the M3 series

270
00:21:29,561 --> 00:21:33,840
was a provision of a kit to convert the weapon from standard .45 caliber

271
00:21:33,841 --> 00:21:36,520
to German 9mm ammunition.

272
00:21:40,160 --> 00:21:44,960
By the end of the war, Guidelamp had made over 600,000 grease guns

273
00:21:44,961 --> 00:21:48,960
and it was replacing the venerable Thompson in ever-increasing numbers.

274
00:21:48,961 --> 00:21:55,580
The introduction of the M3 and M3A1 submachine guns

275
00:21:55,581 --> 00:21:59,140
Marked the passing of an era in military small arms

276
00:21:59,141 --> 00:22:02,540
An age of carefully crafted weapons

277
00:22:02,541 --> 00:22:06,260
With finely milled metal parts and hand-rubbed wood

278
00:22:06,261 --> 00:22:11,140
In the evolving technology of warfare

279
00:22:11,141 --> 00:22:14,800
The lowly grease gun stands as a significant achievement

280
00:22:14,801 --> 00:22:25,300
Between the world wars, the machine gun evolved from essentially a static piece of miniature artillery

281
00:22:25,301 --> 00:22:28,440
to a lightweight, pivotal infantry weapon.

282
00:22:29,040 --> 00:22:34,020
One that could be carried forward in battle to dramatically increase the volume of fire.

283
00:22:36,480 --> 00:22:39,660
Anytime you're engaged with a machine gun, it tends to get your attention, though.

284
00:22:39,661 --> 00:22:45,280
and you can always tell which direction the bullets are coming from.

285
00:22:46,340 --> 00:22:49,100
One man, John Moses Browning,

286
00:22:49,400 --> 00:22:52,420
was responsible for essentially all machine guns

287
00:22:52,421 --> 00:22:55,340
used by American infantry troops in World War II.

288
00:22:57,740 --> 00:23:02,840
Years earlier, the Army had accepted the Browning machine rifle model 1918.

289
00:23:04,200 --> 00:23:06,240
The BAR, as it became known,

290
00:23:06,241 --> 00:23:09,840
entered production too late to be a factor in World War I.

291
00:23:10,840 --> 00:23:16,700
But the U.S. Army used it as the squad-level automatic weapon well into the 1950s.

292
00:23:17,940 --> 00:23:24,100
This is a Browning automatic rifle, a .30 caliber light machine gun, brand new in the box.

293
00:23:24,220 --> 00:23:31,840
It was produced by Northeast Small Arms Company and has never been issued.

294
00:23:31,841 --> 00:23:38,300
It has provisions for mounting a bipod, and the shoulder stock flips up so that it can be rested on the shoulder.

295
00:23:39,340 --> 00:23:47,080
The only material differences between the World War I and the World War II weapons were the bipod and the shoulder stock.

296
00:23:47,700 --> 00:23:50,460
Both weapons saw service in World War II.

297
00:23:52,580 --> 00:23:57,020
The Browning automatic rifle was designed originally as an individual weapon,

298
00:23:57,021 --> 00:24:04,420
but it was so heavy, 20 pounds, that it became used as what's called today a squad automatic weapon or a light machine gun.

299
00:24:06,560 --> 00:24:12,880
The BAR was beautifully crafted with a massive receiver machined from a solid block of steel.

300
00:24:15,600 --> 00:24:23,760
It could be fired from the shoulder or hip, or after 1937, from a prone position using an attached bipod.

301
00:24:27,020 --> 00:24:32,000
While in training, Marine PFC Bill Raines became a designated BAR man

302
00:24:32,001 --> 00:24:36,620
because the flamethrower he was originally assigned to operate made him nauseous.

303
00:24:38,920 --> 00:24:43,940
We were using 100-octane gasoline mixed with diesel oil.

304
00:24:44,680 --> 00:24:48,260
And when I would fire it, it burned away all the oxygen,

305
00:24:48,980 --> 00:24:52,000
and I'd take a big breath, and it just made me deathly ill.

306
00:24:52,700 --> 00:24:56,540
In giving up the flamethrower, I agreed to take the BAR.

307
00:24:57,020 --> 00:25:01,540
and a wonderful weapon, and I never regret doing it.

308
00:25:04,080 --> 00:25:07,640
The BAR had a lot of knockdown power.

309
00:25:08,280 --> 00:25:10,700
It was meant to make the bad guy put their heads down

310
00:25:10,701 --> 00:25:13,860
while the other parts of the squad maneuvered against them.

311
00:25:15,460 --> 00:25:20,500
It would fire 20 rounds where your M1 was only 8 rounds,

312
00:25:20,800 --> 00:25:25,940
and we learned very quickly that the best way to fire it was one man would fire,

313
00:25:25,941 --> 00:25:33,240
and while he reloaded the magazine, the other BAR man would take up the fire and do it in threes.

314
00:25:33,620 --> 00:25:37,540
In that manner, you were laying down a constant field of fire

315
00:25:37,541 --> 00:25:42,900
and not waiting to reload and letting up the fire that was coming down.

316
00:25:44,380 --> 00:25:48,980
Most impressive was the BAR's superior power and penetration ability,

317
00:25:49,760 --> 00:25:54,820
particularly when paired with .30 caliber armor-piercing ammunition, or AP.

318
00:25:56,640 --> 00:26:00,320
In fact, in both the European and Pacific theaters,

319
00:26:00,800 --> 00:26:03,960
AP was used virtually to the exclusion of ball ammo,

320
00:26:04,520 --> 00:26:06,580
the traditional anti-personnel round.

321
00:26:07,200 --> 00:26:09,960
This made the BAR even more deadly.

322
00:26:11,920 --> 00:26:14,700
It was firepower. It was good firepower.

323
00:26:15,620 --> 00:26:17,860
You felt safe with a BAR man in your squad,

324
00:26:18,660 --> 00:26:21,660
because he really was like a miniature 88.

325
00:26:21,661 --> 00:26:30,320
Another Browning-designed weapon would become the infantry's heavy machine gun in World War II.

326
00:26:32,940 --> 00:26:38,680
With a combined gun and tripod weight in excess of 93 pounds, not counting accessories,

327
00:26:39,420 --> 00:26:43,640
the 1917 A1 was indeed a heavy and cumbersome weapon.

328
00:26:44,620 --> 00:26:47,480
Once in position, it tended to remain fixed.

329
00:26:51,660 --> 00:26:58,340
A condenser box was used to prevent steam from the water jacket surrounding the gun barrel from giving away its position.

330
00:27:01,320 --> 00:27:08,580
A sustained rate of fire of 450 to 600 rounds per minute compensated somewhat for its lack of mobility.

331
00:27:08,581 --> 00:27:19,340
from fixed defensive positions the 1917 a1 browning was unsurpassed for laying down

332
00:27:19,341 --> 00:27:22,420
fields of interlacing fire for maximum effect

333
00:27:22,421 --> 00:27:33,600
it's not just willy-nilly they can move elevate and traverse that is bring the weapon up or down

334
00:27:33,601 --> 00:27:38,820
and traverse it left or right to very precisely adjust what's called the beaten zone,

335
00:27:38,960 --> 00:27:41,520
that is where the bullets fall, of the machine gun.

336
00:27:42,120 --> 00:27:47,820
And a good gunner can adjust it vertically, horizontally, or even laterally

337
00:27:47,821 --> 00:27:50,960
to get a beaten zone exactly where he wants it.

338
00:27:52,420 --> 00:27:57,460
The 1917 Browning came to symbolize the machine gun in World War II,

339
00:27:58,000 --> 00:28:02,280
if for no other reason than the public had become familiar with its impressive silhouette.

340
00:28:03,600 --> 00:28:10,160
No less a master than Norman Rockwell immortalized the weapon in a 1942 poster for the War Department,

341
00:28:10,720 --> 00:28:14,540
the only combat painting the artist made during his long career.

342
00:28:18,140 --> 00:28:23,860
The stark arresting image depicts an anonymous machine gunner who could be any mother's son,

343
00:28:24,680 --> 00:28:26,100
holding off an unseen enemy.

344
00:28:27,100 --> 00:28:28,840
He is about to run out of ammunition.

345
00:28:28,841 --> 00:28:33,200
The caption reads,

346
00:28:33,201 --> 00:28:36,080
Let's give them enough, and on time.

347
00:28:37,000 --> 00:28:41,400
It is classic Rockwell, at once poignant and powerful.

348
00:28:43,500 --> 00:28:47,400
Despite the M1917's legendary reputation,

349
00:28:48,080 --> 00:28:50,680
lack of maneuverability remained a drawback.

350
00:28:53,180 --> 00:28:57,600
Lightweight machine guns were needed to provide cover to advancing rifle companies.

351
00:28:58,380 --> 00:29:02,820
Thus, the air-cooled M1919A4 was born.

352
00:29:10,940 --> 00:29:15,300
The gun and its tripod together weighed approximately 45 pounds,

353
00:29:15,820 --> 00:29:18,360
half that of the water-cooled M1917.

354
00:29:19,300 --> 00:29:22,780
While neither a steady nor offering the sustained rate of fire,

355
00:29:23,440 --> 00:29:26,880
the M1919A4 was much easier to set up

356
00:29:26,881 --> 00:29:30,160
and provided a low profile for concealability.

357
00:29:32,360 --> 00:29:39,400
This configuration is a 1919 A4E1, which is used in tanks as a coaxial machine gun.

358
00:29:39,880 --> 00:29:44,300
The cocking handle is to the rear to allow cocking in the confines of a tank.

359
00:29:44,860 --> 00:29:50,180
And this is the 1919 A6 machine gun, which features a bipod and a shoulder stock

360
00:29:50,181 --> 00:29:52,440
and is more portable than the 1919 A4.

361
00:29:52,441 --> 00:30:00,920
covering fire from 30 caliber brownings allowed bill raines and his marine squad to move forward

362
00:30:00,921 --> 00:30:09,620
over the black volcanic sand of iwo jima in february 1945 the machine guns were great you know

363
00:30:09,621 --> 00:30:15,000
they were wonderful to give us support to move up and then we could hold the line while they moved up

364
00:30:15,001 --> 00:30:21,560
and took on new positions the air cooled seemed to be the one the best weapon it was light it was

365
00:30:21,561 --> 00:30:23,200
Mobile, it was easy to carry.

366
00:30:24,840 --> 00:30:26,560
Three men would operate a gun.

367
00:30:26,880 --> 00:30:29,320
Had a gunner, assistant gunner, and an ammo carrier.

368
00:30:29,900 --> 00:30:32,500
So the three would operate a gun very easily.

369
00:30:34,320 --> 00:30:38,880
By and large, the machine guns employed by all sides during World War II

370
00:30:38,881 --> 00:30:42,960
were chambered for .30 caliber rifle ammunition, or its equivalent.

371
00:30:45,820 --> 00:30:51,020
The United States, however, also fielded the heavy Browning .50 caliber machine gun.

372
00:30:51,560 --> 00:31:03,160
John Browning, again, essentially scaled up the 1917 and designed what became known as the M2 machine gun.

373
00:31:04,580 --> 00:31:11,020
Short of cannon fire, the brutal impact of the M2's big bullet was without equal during the war.

374
00:31:12,760 --> 00:31:16,560
A .50 caliber bullet primarily is an anti-material weapon.

375
00:31:17,340 --> 00:31:18,420
It'll take down walls.

376
00:31:18,540 --> 00:31:20,200
It'll take down light armored vehicles.

377
00:31:20,640 --> 00:31:21,920
It'll take down aircraft.

378
00:31:24,260 --> 00:31:26,460
It's just a very, very potent weapon.

379
00:31:27,900 --> 00:31:32,860
The M2 was and remains the most widely used of Browning's machine guns.

380
00:31:34,660 --> 00:31:37,280
It was placed on tanks and half-tracks.

381
00:31:38,740 --> 00:31:41,960
Army and Navy fighter planes used them as offensive armament.

382
00:31:41,961 --> 00:31:50,960
while B-17 and B-24 bombers carried as many as 12 M-2s for defense against enemy fighters.

383
00:31:52,960 --> 00:31:55,780
We used to call it Ma-Deus, M-2.

384
00:31:56,920 --> 00:31:59,620
Ma-Deus because Ma-Deus got everybody's respect.

385
00:32:00,320 --> 00:32:02,340
And there's not much arguing with Ma-Deus either.

386
00:32:03,380 --> 00:32:05,660
When the M-2 machine gun was introduced,

387
00:32:06,280 --> 00:32:09,560
the standard service rifle in the United States was the M-1903 Springfield.

388
00:32:10,500 --> 00:32:15,820
We've gone through the M1, the M1 Garand, the M14, and now we're into the M16.

389
00:32:17,020 --> 00:32:19,980
And the M2 is going to be around for a lot longer.

390
00:32:26,480 --> 00:32:30,660
John Moses Browning, one of America's most prolific gun inventors,

391
00:32:30,661 --> 00:32:34,220
made his first gun out of scrap metal at the age of 13.

392
00:32:34,820 --> 00:32:37,980
U.S. Guns of World War II will return on Modern Marvels.

393
00:32:39,560 --> 00:32:48,020
During the Second World War, the Axis armies issued sidearms primarily as badges of rank for officers.

394
00:32:49,620 --> 00:32:54,380
The Americans, on the other hand, had always viewed pistols as legitimate weapons,

395
00:32:54,940 --> 00:33:00,060
expected to meet the same standards of rugged utility as any other military-issue firearm.

396
00:33:01,300 --> 00:33:04,820
The Colt .45 Automatic triumphed in that role,

397
00:33:04,821 --> 00:33:07,700
performing brilliantly in two world wars

398
00:33:07,701 --> 00:33:12,360
and qualifying in the process as the finest combat handgun in the world.

399
00:33:14,320 --> 00:33:16,860
Officially known as the Model 1911,

400
00:33:17,440 --> 00:33:21,700
the Colt .45 had its origins in the continuing campaign of pacification

401
00:33:21,701 --> 00:33:23,700
in the southern Philippine Islands,

402
00:33:24,200 --> 00:33:26,840
ongoing since the end of the Spanish-American War.

403
00:33:29,020 --> 00:33:32,020
The inadequacies of the .45's predecessor,

404
00:33:32,021 --> 00:33:34,620
the Model 1892 Colt Revolver,

405
00:33:34,820 --> 00:33:39,020
were shockingly demonstrated by the capacity of charging Moral tribesmen

406
00:33:39,021 --> 00:33:42,920
to withstand volley after volley of .38 caliber fire.

407
00:33:44,780 --> 00:33:47,640
The Army decided that it needed a new pistol,

408
00:33:47,840 --> 00:33:50,500
and since the semi-automatic was starting to come around,

409
00:33:51,280 --> 00:33:54,860
John Browning had just designed a new semi-automatic pistol.

410
00:33:55,000 --> 00:33:56,580
This was the Colt Model 1905.

411
00:33:56,740 --> 00:33:58,600
The Army tried these and had a few shortcomings.

412
00:33:59,440 --> 00:34:03,960
And the 1905 was essentially the direct predecessor of the 1911.

413
00:34:04,820 --> 00:34:10,820
Browning and the Colt Firearms Company continued development on the new design.

414
00:34:12,220 --> 00:34:19,120
It was accepted by the Army as Pistol Automatic Caliber .45 Model 1911.

415
00:34:23,700 --> 00:34:30,360
Experience derived in World War I resulted in some minor changes to the shape of the gun being adopted in 1922,

416
00:34:31,140 --> 00:34:34,520
including shortening the trigger and changing the contour of the grip.

417
00:34:34,820 --> 00:34:43,514
With these modifications, the .45 Colt Automatic became the M1911A1 and remained the standard U.S.

418
00:34:43,515 --> 00:34:45,200
sidearm until 1983.

419
00:34:48,120 --> 00:34:51,600
This is a World War II production, 1911A1.

420
00:34:51,980 --> 00:34:56,140
It has the arch mainspring housing, which was changed from flat in 1922.

421
00:34:56,660 --> 00:35:01,780
It has the extended spur here on the grip safety to keep your hand from being bitten by the hammer as it came back.

422
00:35:01,781 --> 00:35:04,800
and it has the cutout here on the side of the frame

423
00:35:04,801 --> 00:35:09,720
to enable you to better reach rounding and grasp the trigger.

424
00:35:10,940 --> 00:35:15,560
Other than that, the gun is pretty much the same as it was back in 1911

425
00:35:15,561 --> 00:35:16,820
when it was originally introduced.

426
00:35:18,060 --> 00:35:22,760
The Model 1911 saw widespread service in every theater of conflict.

427
00:35:23,140 --> 00:35:27,160
Although sometimes criticized for being too heavy and inaccurate,

428
00:35:27,860 --> 00:35:30,660
the .45 stopping power was undeniable.

429
00:35:31,780 --> 00:35:35,820
The pistol is as accurate as the person who's shooting it.

430
00:35:36,500 --> 00:35:40,500
A lot of the inaccuracy stories came from the 60s,

431
00:35:40,940 --> 00:35:44,980
when the .45 pistols that were then in service with the U.S. military

432
00:35:44,981 --> 00:35:47,580
had been around since World War II and were basically worn out.

433
00:35:48,920 --> 00:35:51,680
It's an accurate weapon if you use it right.

434
00:35:52,640 --> 00:35:56,940
One of the most important things in combat firing is the correct grip on the gun stuff.

435
00:35:57,800 --> 00:36:01,720
The way you're supposed to fire a .45 is not just draw it from the hip and shoot it.

436
00:36:01,780 --> 00:36:03,840
because it had an awful kick

437
00:36:03,841 --> 00:36:07,560
and you come down on your target with a .45

438
00:36:07,561 --> 00:36:09,660
and you can't miss.

439
00:36:15,140 --> 00:36:17,760
.45s were issued to officers and sergeants,

440
00:36:18,020 --> 00:36:20,000
machine gun crews, tank crews,

441
00:36:20,420 --> 00:36:23,520
anyone whose primary job prevented him from carrying a rifle.

442
00:36:24,100 --> 00:36:27,360
But pistols of all kinds were sought after by everyone.

443
00:36:27,361 --> 00:36:32,000
I've been told that the chaplains even carried a .45.

444
00:36:33,000 --> 00:36:34,740
Everybody cherished a pistol.

445
00:36:35,740 --> 00:36:37,060
Everybody wanted a pistol.

446
00:36:37,560 --> 00:36:39,460
And they rode home for pistols.

447
00:36:40,340 --> 00:36:42,020
But pistols were great.

448
00:36:42,140 --> 00:36:49,180
We had a .38 caliber Smith & Wesson that was carried by our guide sergeant in my platoon.

449
00:36:49,940 --> 00:36:53,000
And it was passed down through eight or ten men.

450
00:36:53,001 --> 00:36:57,340
as one would get hit, wounded, or would be killed,

451
00:36:57,680 --> 00:36:59,380
it would come down to somebody else.

452
00:37:00,160 --> 00:37:04,520
And that went through ten hands before the campaign was over.

453
00:37:04,940 --> 00:37:09,140
The value of that pistol came to light at night when you were dug in

454
00:37:09,141 --> 00:37:14,000
to be able to lay there and feel the security of that pistol in your hand

455
00:37:14,001 --> 00:37:16,840
that if somebody climbed up to your hole and tried to get in,

456
00:37:16,841 --> 00:37:20,440
you had something that you very quickly could defend yourself with.

457
00:37:24,000 --> 00:37:33,040
The blitzkrieg tactics used by the Germans early in the war demonstrated that Allied rear echelon personnel could be exposed to enemy action.

458
00:37:34,040 --> 00:37:39,620
What was needed, the argument went, was something other than .45s to arm support troops.

459
00:37:42,280 --> 00:37:52,580
In June 1940, the Army issued specifications for a light rifle that could take the place of both the .45 pistol and the submachine gun.

460
00:37:53,000 --> 00:38:01,460
In September 1941, it was announced that the Winchester Repeating Arms Company of New Haven, Connecticut, had won the design competition.

461
00:38:02,120 --> 00:38:07,080
The Army adopted the new weapon as carbine caliber .30 M1.

462
00:38:09,620 --> 00:38:17,640
The M1 carbine was designed to issue to people who don't shoot for a living, cooks, clerks, mechanics, officers, NCOs,

463
00:38:17,700 --> 00:38:20,620
and it was designed to replace a pistol rather than a rifle.

464
00:38:20,621 --> 00:38:25,460
and it served through World War II, Korea, and it was still in service during the Vietnam War.

465
00:38:28,400 --> 00:38:31,360
The carbine's compactness earned it early favor,

466
00:38:31,361 --> 00:38:38,340
but in combat, the small .30 caliber round lacked the knockdown power of either the .30-odd-6 at distance

467
00:38:38,341 --> 00:38:40,720
or the .45 at close range.

468
00:38:44,160 --> 00:38:47,380
Despite the ambivalence surrounding the carbine's effectiveness,

469
00:38:48,020 --> 00:38:50,820
over 6 million were produced by the end of the war.

470
00:38:52,460 --> 00:38:58,780
No less than 10 manufacturing plants as diverse as Winchester, IBM, General Motors,

471
00:38:59,200 --> 00:39:04,060
National Postal Meter, and the Roccola Jukebox Corporation churn them out,

472
00:39:04,540 --> 00:39:07,460
the most of any World War II personal weapon.

473
00:39:09,720 --> 00:39:14,780
But ultimately, the tale of this gun and all others that served the infantry

474
00:39:14,781 --> 00:39:19,500
cannot be recounted without considering the most vital component of any weapon,

475
00:39:20,120 --> 00:39:20,820
the soldier.

476
00:39:22,940 --> 00:39:24,960
For no matter how great the weapon,

477
00:39:25,440 --> 00:39:28,540
it was just so much junk unless the infantrymen,

478
00:39:29,020 --> 00:39:32,620
resisting every impulse to the contrary, returned fire.

479
00:39:33,920 --> 00:39:38,140
You could pinpoint the guys in your mind that were aggressive,

480
00:39:38,820 --> 00:39:39,680
that led the way.

481
00:39:39,780 --> 00:39:41,480
That's why they say lead the way rangers.

482
00:39:42,080 --> 00:39:43,880
There were certain people that led the way.

483
00:39:43,881 --> 00:39:45,240
and you followed.

484
00:39:46,140 --> 00:39:48,440
Our company commander, our lieutenants,

485
00:39:48,620 --> 00:39:49,500
the squad leaders,

486
00:39:50,160 --> 00:39:52,460
whoever were there in charge led the way

487
00:39:52,461 --> 00:39:53,161
and we followed.

488
00:39:54,080 --> 00:39:55,420
And that's why we were Rangers.

489
00:39:56,700 --> 00:39:58,760
Because we had the balls to do what they did.

490
00:40:00,840 --> 00:40:02,240
For three months,

491
00:40:02,620 --> 00:40:05,040
Harry Davis, the Ranger who came ashore

492
00:40:05,041 --> 00:40:06,060
at Omaha Beach,

493
00:40:06,600 --> 00:40:08,540
fought mile by mile to enlarge

494
00:40:08,541 --> 00:40:11,040
the tenuous foothold the Allies had established

495
00:40:11,041 --> 00:40:12,180
on Fortress Europe.

496
00:40:13,880 --> 00:40:22,380
On September 15, 1944, his 101st day in combat, Davis was grievously wounded by a German mortar shell.

497
00:40:23,780 --> 00:40:28,760
His wife in Philadelphia mistakenly received a telegram that her husband had been killed in action.

498
00:40:31,400 --> 00:40:35,060
Weeks passed before she learned that he was alive.

499
00:40:35,061 --> 00:40:47,400
Five months later, on the other side of the world, the 2nd Battalion of the 28th Marine Regiment was about to rendezvous with history on the island of Iwo Jima.

500
00:40:49,620 --> 00:40:53,660
This was my first combat, and I had to pick one of the biggest.

501
00:40:55,720 --> 00:40:59,000
You know, we all lived with fear, there was no question about that.

502
00:40:59,001 --> 00:41:03,360
And, you know, to have these close friends that you had been with for over a year,

503
00:41:03,900 --> 00:41:05,700
just all of a sudden they're gone.

504
00:41:06,340 --> 00:41:07,880
They're no longer with you. They're dead.

505
00:41:09,740 --> 00:41:13,560
And to be able to shift that off and clear your mind of it

506
00:41:13,561 --> 00:41:17,520
and know that the objective was at the end and you had to keep going.

507
00:41:17,620 --> 00:41:18,620
You didn't have a choice.

508
00:41:19,140 --> 00:41:20,880
It was just keep going.

509
00:41:23,340 --> 00:41:27,620
The fighting on Iwo Jima was supposed to be over in 72 hours.

510
00:41:28,540 --> 00:41:31,400
The Japanese held out for 37 days.

511
00:41:34,540 --> 00:41:39,560
7,000 Marines died on eight square miles of volcanic rock and sand.

512
00:41:40,680 --> 00:41:44,980
One of every three Americans who fought there was killed or wounded.

513
00:41:48,200 --> 00:41:51,780
On February 23rd, five days into the battle,

514
00:41:51,781 --> 00:41:57,640
Reynas' first squad was ordered to take a large American flag to the summit of Mount Suribachi,

515
00:41:58,260 --> 00:42:01,500
which had just been wrested from the Japanese after heavy fighting.

516
00:42:03,700 --> 00:42:10,400
The order came just as Reynas, ever the conscientious Marine, had disassembled his BAR for cleaning.

517
00:42:11,160 --> 00:42:14,340
As his squad left without him and moved up the mountain,

518
00:42:14,341 --> 00:42:19,420
Rainis had no idea that six of his buddies were about to be immortalized

519
00:42:19,421 --> 00:42:23,660
in the image that has come to symbolize victory in the Second World War.

520
00:42:29,500 --> 00:42:35,740
On the 2nd of March, 1945, nine days after the flag raising on Mount Suribachi,

521
00:42:36,560 --> 00:42:39,980
PFC Bill Rainis was hit in the chest by mortar shrapnel.

522
00:42:39,981 --> 00:42:48,840
Of the original ten men in his squad, only one, Ira Hayes, made it through the entire campaign unscathed.

523
00:42:50,480 --> 00:43:01,080
And Ira Hayes, a quiet Pima Indian from Arizona, is captured forever, reaching into the sky at the Marine Corps Memorial in Arlington, Virginia.

524
00:43:01,081 --> 00:43:09,960
that's one of the most outstanding things in our capital is to be able to go there and stand and

525
00:43:09,961 --> 00:43:17,800
look up at that beautiful bronze statue and see it but it's hard for me to stand there and look at it

526
00:43:17,801 --> 00:43:26,600
without thinking of mike strength frank sowsley harlan block ira hayes john bradley because i was

527
00:43:26,601 --> 00:43:27,600
so close to them.

528
00:43:29,640 --> 00:43:32,140
All six of those men are gone now.

529
00:43:34,740 --> 00:43:37,440
The American infantryman in World War II

530
00:43:37,441 --> 00:43:39,840
fought with a great confidence

531
00:43:39,841 --> 00:43:42,400
that he had the entire backing

532
00:43:42,401 --> 00:43:43,920
of the arsenal of democracy.

533
00:43:44,720 --> 00:43:46,960
He saw huge air armadas

534
00:43:46,961 --> 00:43:48,720
overflying him on the way to Berlin

535
00:43:48,721 --> 00:43:49,960
to pound the enemy's homeland.

536
00:43:50,820 --> 00:43:53,140
He knew a tremendous logistical force

537
00:43:53,141 --> 00:43:54,900
was in place to support him.

538
00:43:54,901 --> 00:43:59,040
He knew that a great American navy controlled the Atlantic and Pacific.

539
00:43:59,920 --> 00:44:08,340
But despite all that, the infantrymen realized that the ultimate job of closing with the enemy and destroying them

540
00:44:08,341 --> 00:44:14,680
and getting to Berlin and to Tokyo ultimately fell on his back.

541
00:44:15,260 --> 00:44:21,040
And the fact that they did this with the weapons that they were provided, the tactics in which they had trained,

542
00:44:21,041 --> 00:44:25,520
and that they did this despite taking very heavy casualties

543
00:44:25,521 --> 00:44:28,500
is a great tribute to the American fighting.

